# UI/UX — components

`context/instructions/ui-components.md` is the reference for building or changing
a component: its states, variants, composition and the details around it. Read it
with `context/instructions/ui.md` (the doctrine),
`context/instructions/ui-accessibility.md` (interaction and review) and
`context/instructions/ui-tokens.md` (the values it uses).

## States

- For every interactive element, enumerate the states — default, hover, focus,
  active, disabled, loading, error, selected — render the ones that apply, and
  say why the others do not.
- `focus-visible` must be visible for keyboard users. Never remove focus
  styling without an equally visible replacement.
- A state you did not render is a decision; state it rather than leaving it
  undefined.

## Variants and sizes

- Keep a small, closed set: variants differ by **intent** (primary, secondary,
  destructive), sizes by **density**. Do not multiply variants for one-off looks.
- A new variant is a design decision, not a styling shortcut; if an existing one
  fits, use it.

## Composition

- Compose from the project's existing primitives; do not fork a component to
  change one property.
- Prefer slots/children and composition over a growing list of boolean props.
- One component, one concern: a control that also lays out a page is two things.

## Blocks

A **block** is a reusable composition unit assembled from components — a section,
a card, a form row, an empty state. It is the unit a project reuses across
surfaces.

- Reuse a block instead of re-creating it; a new block is a design decision, like
  a new variant.
- A block **declares what it is compatible with** — the variants, sizes and slots
  it accepts and the tokens it uses — so it can be reused without surprise.
- **Containment:** a block owns its internal layout; the surrounding page owns
  the space between blocks. A block does not reach outside its box (global styles,
  shared mutable state, positioning against a foreign ancestor).
- **Approval never transfers:** approving a block approves the variants it
  declares, nothing more. A one-off tweak stays a one-off; promote it into the
  block and re-approve rather than diverging silently.
- Keep the block vocabulary stable across artifacts (see `ui-taste.md`, Block
  coherence).

## Focus management

- Focus always goes somewhere predictable. On open, move it into the new
  surface; on close, restore it to the trigger.
- Never trap the keyboard without an escape; a modal must be escapable.
- Never let an action remove the focused element without moving focus.

## Disabled, loading, error

- **Disabled** — not available now: non-interactive, and say why if it is not
  obvious.
- **Loading** — in progress: keep the layout stable and prefer a skeleton over a
  spinner inside content, so the page does not jump.
- **Error** — name the problem and the recovery, and keep the user's input.
  Never clear a form on failure.

## Empty states

- First-run empty (nothing exists yet) differs from no-results (a filter matched
  nothing). Say which, and what to do next.
- An empty state is a designed state, not an absence.

## Overlays

- An overlay must escape `overflow` ancestors — use `dialog`, popover or a
  portal; a positioned element inside a clipped container will be cut off.
- Modal is a last resort: prefer inline or a non-blocking popover when the task
  does not need to block the page.

## The parts you did not draw

Check the cheap details that read as built rather than assembled: text
selection and caret colour, scrollbar styling, focus ring, underline offset,
tabular numerals in data. They are stack-neutral and high-signal.

## Functional copy

- Controls name their action ("Save changes", not "Submit").
- Errors name the problem and the recovery.
- Microcopy states what will happen; it does not hedge or decorate.

## Boundary

- Visual direction, tone of voice and coherence live in
  `context/instructions/ui-taste.md`.
- Contrast, target size, reduced motion and the review checklist live in
  `context/instructions/ui-accessibility.md`.
- Concrete values come from tokens (`context/instructions/ui-tokens.md`); how
  they map to a framework is the adapter contract
  (`context/instructions/ui-adapters.md`).
